WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.560
Where does rain go after it lands on your street?

2
00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:09.160
Watch a puddle after a storm and you will see water moving downhill.

3
00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:11.260
Some rain soaks into the ground.

4
00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:14.660
When the ground is full or hard, the rest runs downhill.

5
00:00:16.383 --> 00:00:20.023
In the activity, your child crumples paper into hills and valleys.

6
00:00:20.643 --> 00:00:23.383
One low corner is coloured blue and called the bay.

7
00:00:23.963 --> 00:00:25.563
The high ground is called a ridge.

8
00:00:26.183 --> 00:00:28.423
Rain on one side of a ridge flows one way.

9
00:00:29.043 --> 00:00:31.003
Rain on the other side flows the other way.

10
00:00:31.767 --> 00:00:36.547
All the land that drains to the same stream, river or bay is called a watershed.

11
00:00:37.027 --> 00:00:39.647
A ridge splits one watershed from the next.

12
00:00:40.047 --> 00:00:44.507
If water pools in more than one low spot on the paper, your paper has more than one

13
00:00:44.507 --> 00:00:45.507
watershed.

14
00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:49.910
Little streams join bigger streams, and bigger streams join rivers.

15
00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:53.530
As water runs across the land, it picks things up.

16
00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:57.570
Soil, litter, pet waste and lawn fertilizer can all go along.

17
00:00:58.230 --> 00:00:59.670
Much of it can end up downstream.

18
00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:04.830
In most places, storm drains carry rain straight to a creek without cleaning it.

19
00:01:05.483 --> 00:01:08.483
More than half of Virginia drains to the Chesapeake Bay.

20
00:01:09.063 --> 00:01:13.043
Most of the rest drains south to the sounds of North Carolina or west toward the

21
00:01:13.043 --> 00:01:17.443
Gulf of Mexico. A map of Virginia's watersheds helps you find your town.

22
00:01:18.217 --> 00:01:23.137
Virginia's grade four standard asks children to model a watershed and name the one

23
00:01:23.137 --> 00:01:27.997
they live in. It also asks them to explain: we all live downstream.

24
00:01:28.817 --> 00:01:33.377
If your home is in the Bay watershed, rain that runs off your yard can reach it.

25
00:01:33.757 --> 00:01:35.777
It travels through creeks and rivers.

26
00:01:36.377 --> 00:01:39.957
Your child can trace which way rain runs from your yard on a map.

27
00:01:40.357 --> 00:01:45.457
For a younger child, one idea is enough: what we put on the land can reach the

28
00:01:45.457 --> 00:01:47.957
water. The safety notes are on the page.
